WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:06.670
A world filled with reverent statues, convoluted
family trees, and mischievous halflings.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:12.568
Episode 3 of "The Rings of Power" has more
of Tolkien's lore at work than you may have recognized.

00:00:12.851 --> 00:00:16.880
Anyone who's watched Peter Jackson's "The
Lord of the Rings" trilogy is familiar with

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.500
the epic scene in "The Return of the King"
where Pippin lights the beacons of Gondor.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.300
The little Halfling scales a watchtower and
tosses a flame onto a pile of brushwood without

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.760
the guards seeing him.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.430
This sets off a string of similar fire-bound
watchtowers, which send a visual message to

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.929
King Théoden in Rohan that Gondor calls for
aid.

00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:39.780
The scene is also mirrored in the opening
chapter of "The Return of the King" book,

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.620
although in this case, Pippin observes the
lit beacons from afar rather than lighting

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.620
them himself.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.839
"The beacons of Minas Tirith!

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:50.430
The beacons are lit!

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.019
Gondor calls for aid!"

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:56.769
In either case, the opportunity to light some
beacons is too good to miss, and it should

00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:01.059
come as no surprise that "The Rings of Power"
found its own way to sneak some lit beacons

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:02.589
into its adaptation.

00:01:02.589 --> 00:01:07.570
In Episode 3, when Galadriel and Halbrand
arrive in Númenor on Elendil's ship, they

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.630
pass a watchtower out at sea.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.640
As they look up, lo and behold, a beacon is
burning.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.340
In this case, it's likely a lighthouse beacon
designed for ships, but the callback to Peter

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.849
Jackson's films is difficult to miss.

00:01:19.163 --> 00:01:22.330
The Elves aren't the only ones with special
statues.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:27.259
While Lindon is littered with gorgeous wooden
carvings, Númenor has a lot of its own sculptures,

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:29.219
mostly chiseled right into stone.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:33.840
In Episode 3, as Elendil's ship sails down
the narrow waterway that leads into the heart

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.259
of his home island, viewers can see giant
statues of heads perched along the route.

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:42.710
Full-blown forms are also shown, including
a gigantic statue just as the ship arrives

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:43.729
at the main port.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:48.469
This last statue was a central point of speculation
for months after it first showed up in the

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:51.670
show's original Super Bowl ad in early 2022.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.719
Guesses as to the statue's identity spanned
the gamut.

00:01:54.719 --> 00:01:57.490
Númenor has a lot of kings and queens after
all.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.659
But there are a couple of details that make
one regal figure the clear frontrunner.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:05.580
First, there's a giant statue of a bird next
to the humanoid figure.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.840
Second, if you look closely, there appears
to be a small item bound to the figure's forehead.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.560
This indicates that we're looking at a statue
of Eärendil, one of Tolkien's most important

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.730
Middle-earth heroes.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.540
His wife is Elwing, who is turned into a bird
at one point.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.769
He's also the father of Elrond and his twin
brother Elros, who is the first king of Númenor.

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:25.980
And the relic on his forehead?

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.730
Eärendil ends up sailing through the heavens
on his ship with one of the shining jewels,

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.640
the Silmarils, on his brow.

00:02:32.815 --> 00:02:37.700
When Isildur first shows up, it's early in
Episode 3 when we see the young cadet preparing

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.910
for the sea trials with his shipmates.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.681
When the exercise is completed, they sail
to shore and stand, facing the water, while

00:02:43.681 --> 00:02:48.997
the drill sergeant blows on a horn and pays
homage to the massive body of water before him.

00:02:48.997 --> 00:02:51.790
"The sea is always right!"

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.000
"The sea is always right!"

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.370
The Easter egg here is tucked into the drill
sergeant's accessories, specifically, his

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.640
shell-shaped musical instrument.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.670
The choice of a shell isn't just a fun way
to show off the Númenórean penchant for

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.830
sea travel.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.159
It's a detail that connects right back to
one of the most important beings in all of

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:12.709
Middle-earth: Ulmo.

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:17.360
Ulmo is one of the Valar, the angelic beings
who function as guardians of Middle-earth.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.720
Ulmo is called the Lord of Waters, and his
power extends throughout all of the oceans,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.400
seas, and tributaries of the world.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.230
And what does a being as epic as Ulmo, the
Lord of Waters use to get some attention?

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.469
Why giant sea shells, of course.

00:03:31.469 --> 00:03:35.360
"The Silmarillion" says that, at times, Ulmo
will come to Middle-earth and

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.610
"there make music upon his great horns, the
Ulumúri, that are wrought of white shell;

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.700
and those to whom that music comes hear it
ever after in their hearts, and longing for

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.684
the sea never leaves them again."

00:03:47.036 --> 00:03:52.159
When we arrive in Númenor in Episode 3, there
are a lot of sights to take in all at once.

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:55.739
One of these is a stunningly vibrant tree
with white petals.

00:03:55.739 --> 00:04:00.680
The concept of a white tree isn't new to Tolkien
fans, even casual viewers of the films.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.310
In Peter Jackson's "The Return of the King,"
a white tree can be seen in the center of

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:09.220
a court in Minas Tirith, except this time,
we're looking at a corpse, not a thriving,

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.220
living organism.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.459
The two images aren't just similar, either.

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:14.150
They're actually related.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:18.510
That's right, the dead White Tree of Gondor
can trace its roots back to its ancestor seen

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.500
in Númenor in "The Rings of Power."

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:25.040
The Númenórean iteration of the arboreal
wonder is called Nimloth, and it's brought

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.820
to Númenor by the Elves as a gift from Valinor.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.260
Eventually, Isildur takes a fruit of Nimloth
with him to Middle-earth, where he plants

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.010
it in Minas Ithil.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:38.140
When that fortress is captured and becomes
the headquarters of the Nazgûl, the tree

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:42.575
is destroyed, but one of its fruits survives
and is planted in Minas Tirith.

00:04:42.714 --> 00:04:48.570
In "The Rings of Power," Númenor is led by
Queen Regent Míriel when Galadriel and Halbrand

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:49.570
first arrive.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:55.610
In Episode 3, Míriel talks to Elendil about
the struggle that her people have with trusting Elves.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:01.058
"Though elves have been unwelcome on our shores since
the reign of my grandfather's great-grandfather."

00:05:01.058 --> 00:05:06.060
This feels like an eloquently written throwaway
line meant to emphasize how serious it is

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.540
that Elendil brought an Elf back to Númenor,
but as with all things Tolkien, things go

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.130
much deeper than that.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:16.610
The author traced the entire line of Númenórean
kings in his writings, which means we can

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:21.350
take a quick stroll back up the family tree
to see who Míriel's "grandfather's great-grandfather

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.940
is," and it turns out that it's a not-so-nice
fellow named Ar-Adûnakhôr.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.630
In the book "Unfinished Tales," Tolkien explains
that

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:33.160
"In this reign the Elven-tongues were no longer
used, nor permitted to be taught, but were

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.690
maintained in secret by the Faithful; and
the ships from Eressëa came seldom and secretly

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.170
to the west shores of Númenor thereafter."

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:45.530
When Míriel specifically highlights that
her great-great-great grandfather started

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:50.990
the antipathy toward the Elves, it isn't a
line that the writers created out of whole cloth.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.730
It's a deliberate reference to a grumpy Númenórean
king who ruled long before the "Rings of Power"

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.451
story ever starts.

00:05:57.768 --> 00:06:02.650
When Galadriel visits the Western regions
of Númenor with Elendil, the pair of travelers

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:07.660
visit the Hall of Lore, a library and depository
of information that Elros, the first king

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.840
of Númenor, himself created.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.490
While the actual hall is made up for the show,
Elros is very much a Tolkien original.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:18.320
He's the brother of Elrond and a character
that comes from a very complicated family

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.150
tree that includes Elves, Men, and even the
angelic Maiar.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:26.660
While Elrond chooses the immortal life of
an Elf, though, Elros opts for the mortality

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.020
of being a Man.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:32.290
Along with choosing the mystery of the gift
of death, Elros also becomes a mighty king

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:35.440
who founds Númenor and lives for five centuries.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.530
By the time of "The Rings of Power," Elros
has been gone for a long while, even as his

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:43.610
twin brother still serves as Gil-galad's herald
back in Lindon.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:48.660
This leads to an interesting line when Galadriel
and Elendil look at a tapestry of the twin

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.020
brothers in the Hall of Lore.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:54.110
Galadriel confirms that she knew Elros, but
makes an interesting statement.

00:06:54.917 --> 00:06:56.707
"But I was always closer with his brother."

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:03.060
For savvy viewers, this is a quiet connection
to Galadriel's already established friendship

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.060
with Elrond.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.190
But it goes even deeper.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:09.010
In the source material, Elrond eventually
marries Galadriel's daughter, and the two

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:14.250
will go on to serve on the White Council and
generally resist Sauron side by side over

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.250
the millennia.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:18.301
To say she's closer to Elrond is an understatement.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:23.290
Early in Season 1, the Harfoot leader Sadoc
Burrows tells an endlessly inquisitive Nori

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.750
Brandyfoot that the stars are strange.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:30.140
The foreboding words materialize in the form
of the Stranger, who crash lands from the

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.860
sky right near the Harfoot community.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.910
Nori takes the Stranger under her tiny yet
formidable wing and, in Episode 3, it comes

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.150
out that she's been helping the odd super-powered
giant.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:45.150
In the fallout, Sadoc faces off against Nori,
reprimanding her in front of the entire clan.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:50.100
At one point, Nori's father Largo chimes in,
pointing out that the whole situation is extraordinary

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:53.580
before asking if he'd ever seen beings falling
from the stars.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.880
Sadoc has an interesting response.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:08:02.020
"I've heard of beings that were turned into
stars, but never the other way around."

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:06.660
The reference to beings becoming stars feels
random, but once again, it could be a quiet

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.100
Easter egg pointing to Tolkien's famous sailor
of the firmament, Eärendil.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:16.260
The hero ends his epic First Age career by
having a shining Silmaril bound to his forehead

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:20.380
before his ship sails off into the heavens
over Middle-earth, in effect, turning him

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:21.620
into a star.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:26.010
The thought that a random Harfoot leader in
an isolated community would somehow know about

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:30.780
Eärendil seems unlikely, but the line could
still be left behind for those to find it

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:31.780
who can.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:32.780
Nori knows she isn't special

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:36.879
Toward the end of Episode 3, we see Nori's
mother, Marigold, confront her about her actions

00:08:36.879 --> 00:08:41.180
with the Stranger, asking if she thinks it's
destiny and that she's somehow special.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:43.550
The little Harfoot's response is telling.

00:08:44.805 --> 00:08:47.270
"I know I'm not special.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.216
I know I'm just one little Harfoot in a grand,
wide world."

00:08:52.216 --> 00:08:58.339
For fans of Tolkien's canon, the line echoes
another spoken by Gandalf at the end of "The Hobbit."

00:08:58.339 --> 00:09:02.069
On the last page of that book, Gandalf and
some of the Dwarves come back to visit their

00:09:02.069 --> 00:09:04.180
burglar friend in the Shire.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:08.320
Bilbo expresses surprise that so many things
they hoped to happen had come true, at which

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.199
point the Grey Wizard interjects,

00:09:10.199 --> 00:09:12.227
"And why should not they prove true?

00:09:12.227 --> 00:09:17.179
Surely you don't disbelieve the prophesies,
because you had a hand in bringing them about yourself?"

00:09:17.179 --> 00:09:20.959
Gandalf goes on to highlight the larger purpose
behind Bilbo's story, saying,

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:25.680
"You don't really suppose, do you, that all
your adventures and escapes were managed by

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.250
mere luck, just for your sole benefit?

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:32.860
You are a very fine person, Mr. Baggins, and
I am very fond of you; but you are only quite

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:35.660
a little fellow in a wide world, after all."

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:39.769
This concept of a higher purpose runs throughout
Tolkien's stories, and it's cool to see the

00:09:39.769 --> 00:09:43.758
larger theme reflected in Nori's small-yet-significant
story.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:48.620
When Galadriel visits Halbrand in prison,
the center of the room is filled with a giant

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:53.389
statue of a blue, mermaid-looking female figure
positioned as if she's swimming.

00:09:53.389 --> 00:09:58.203
It's an odd sight in a jail, but it may be
there to help calm the occupants in their cells.

00:09:58.203 --> 00:09:58.719
Why?

00:09:58.719 --> 00:10:03.449
Because it looks an awful lot like the Maia
Uinen, an angelic being known for bringing

00:10:03.449 --> 00:10:06.060
peace and calm to turbulent waters.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:11.595
Uinen is married to the violent Maia Ossë,
who has a reputation for causing storms at sea.

00:10:11.595 --> 00:10:15.750
"The Silmarillion" describes Uinen as the
counterpoint to her spouse, adding,

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:20.449
"[Ossë's] spouse is Uinen, the Lady of the
Seas, whose hair lies spread through all waters

00:10:20.449 --> 00:10:21.459
under sky.

00:10:21.459 --> 00:10:25.970
All creatures she loves that live in the salt
streams, and all weeds that grow there; to

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:31.509
her mariners cry, for she can lay calm upon
the waves, restraining the wildness of Ossë."

00:10:31.509 --> 00:10:33.480
The flattering description ends with the line,

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:38.399
"The Númenóreans lived long in her protection,
and held her in reverence equal to the Valar."

00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:42.670
While it could just be a random statue, the
fact that she's loved by the Men of Númenor

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:46.949
and even details like the seaweed in her hair
seem to reinforce the idea that we're looking

00:10:46.949 --> 00:10:50.318
at an Easter egg visualization of the Lady
of the Seas.

